    "lawCode": "Add §496, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3315\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to requiring\nnotice of intention to sell exempt real property\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe goal of this legislation is to give county and local governments,\nschool districts and other public entities timely notice of the\nintention to sell tax exempt real property. Such notice will allow\nlocal governments to compete with other potential buyers for\nacquisition of the property, if they are interested.  Compliance,\nrather than monetary fines, is emphasized in this legislation.\nNon-compliance or late compliance would be considered a barrier to or\ndelay the sale.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nAmends the Real Property Tax Law by adding a new section four hundred\nninety-six to provide for notification of intention to sell private\nproperty exempt from real property taxation to those local\njurisdictions providing such real property tax exemption.\n\nThis legislation includes private property exempt from real property\ntaxation because of the not-for-profit status of the property owner\n(Section 420a-nonprofit organizations; mandatory class and Section\n420b-nonprofit organizations; permissive class). Also included are\ngovernmental entities not subject to real property tax payment\n\n(Section 404-State of New York, Section 406-Municipal corporations,\nSection 408- School districts and boards of cooperative educational\nservices, Section 41 O-Special districts, Section 410a-Special\ndistricts, Section 410b-Special water districts, Section 412- Public\nauthorities, Section 412a- Industrial development agencies, Section\n412b-Industrial development agency railroad property, Section\n414-Public housing; publicly assisted housing and limited-profit\nnursing homes).\n\nThese exempt property owners must notify, by certified mail, each\npublic entity from whose taxation the owner is exempt of the intention\nto sell such property. The notification must be received by the public\nentity at least thirty (30) days before the exempt property is\nadvertised for sale, or, if the property is not advertised for sale,\nat least thirty (30) days before the contract of sale is signed.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, the value of tax exempt property in New York State exceeds\n$165 billion. The burden of exemptions is usually carried by the\nnonexempt taxpayers and local governments providing the exemption.\nThe transfer of tax exempt property often involves issues of local\nland use policy, environmental, development and public planning\nconcerns.  This legislation merely provides local governments with\nnotice of intention to sell, so that they have the ability to compete\nfor the acquisition of tax exempt property, if it serves their\ninterests. No special treatment is accorded these governments and no\nburden is placed on the owners of the property as to sale.\n\nThe loss of revenues provided by real property tax exemptions should\njustify notice to local governments of the ability to bargain for tax\nexempt properties. Notice gives them this opportunity.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :  This act shall take effect on the sixtieth day\nafter it shall become a law.\n",
